GLOSSARY=[
('Supervised learning','Learn a mapping from examples whose correct answers are known. Here, listing attributes are inputs and price_tier is the answer.','load'),
('Classification vs regression','Classification predicts a label. Regression predicts a numeric quantity. This task predicts a tier, even though the labels are written as numbers.','load'),
('Training / validation / test','Fitting learns model state, tuning guides choices, audit checks the frozen choice, and competition test labels remain hidden. “Validation” can mean tune or audit, so say which one.','split'),
('Inference','Apply learned preprocessing and a fitted model to new rows to obtain predictions. Inference should not fit new medians or learn a new vocabulary on the test rows.','refit'),
('Generalization','Performance on new examples. Our profile-group holdout measures transfer to similar listings, not a guarantee for a completely new city or operator.','audit'),
('Bias and variance','A restrictive model can miss real patterns (bias); an overly flexible one can fit unstable training details (variance). Capacity controls and validation help balance this trade-off.','models'),
('Overfitting','Fitting training-specific patterns that do not transfer equally well. The chosen LightGBM has training F1 0.9443 versus tune F1 0.7355, so its strong training score is not its expected new-row score.','models'),
('Underfitting','The model or representation cannot fit useful structure well. Similar low training and tuning scores can suggest underfitting, but are not a complete diagnosis on their own.','models'),
('Imputation','Fill missing values by a learned rule. The baseline pipeline uses fitting-set numeric medians and can add missingness indicators. Native boosting inputs follow different missing-value handling.','features'),
('Standardization','Subtract a learned mean and divide by a learned scale. It makes numeric feature scales more comparable for the linear baseline. It does not make the data normally distributed.','features'),
('Log transform','log1p(x)=log(1+x) compresses large nonnegative values while leaving 0 defined. The builder uses it for many counts and uses a signed version for time intervals.','features'),
('Quantile clipping','Cap large values using a fitting-set quantile instead of deleting every unusual row. Our term caps use 0.995; above-cap flags preserve the fact that a value exceeded its cap.','features'),
('One-hot encoding','Create indicator columns for nominal categories. Unseen values are handled by the fitted encoder’s policy; they must not silently change the feature layout.','features'),
('Multi-hot encoding','Several indicator columns may be 1 for the same row. That matches a listing with multiple facilities, unlike a single-category field.','features'),
('Unknown category','A value absent when the vocabulary was fitted. The baseline encoder ignores unknowns; LightGBM frames convert out-of-vocabulary categories to missing. CatBoost has its own native handling.','features'),
('Feature engineering','Create representations such as date spans, total age in months, facilities and capacity ratios. Useful meaning and honest fitting matter more than merely adding columns.','features'),
('Feature selection','Choose a subset of inputs. The final notebook did not deploy the separate M3 L1-selected 200-input classifier; its selected LightGBM uses the engineered frame.','models'),
('L1 vs L2 regularization','L1 encourages sparse coefficients; L2 discourages large weights. The final SGD baseline uses L2 by default, while LightGBM specifies both reg_alpha and reg_lambda.','models'),
('Decision tree','Recursively divide inputs using feature-based conditions. A leaf provides an output for rows reaching it. The guide’s schematic trees illustrate the idea, not dumped thresholds from our fitted model.','models'),
('Tree depth vs leaf count','Depth counts decisions along a path; leaves count terminal regions. num_leaves=63 is a capacity limit per tree, not 63 models or depth 63.','models'),
('Extra Trees','An ensemble of randomized decision trees. It randomizes split candidates more strongly than a conventional random forest. Our setting uses 80 trees, depth at most 20, and at least 5 samples per leaf.','models'),
('Bagging vs boosting','Independent randomized estimators combine their predictions; boosting builds additions sequentially to improve the current loss. Extra Trees is in the first broad ensemble family but does not bootstrap by default here.','models'),
('Gradient boosting','Fit successive trees to loss-gradient information and add their contributions with a learning-rate scale. For this classification model, the loss is multiclass log loss.','models'),
('LightGBM','Histogram-based gradient-boosted trees with native categorical support. Our selected setting has 63 leaves, minimum child support 50 and learning rate 0.04.','models'),
('CatBoost','Gradient-boosted trees with native processing for categorical variables. Our notebook passes categorical strings explicitly and fills missing numeric values with −999 for this branch.','models'),
('Learning rate','Shrink each boosting update. A smaller update often needs more rounds; reducing the rate alone does not guarantee better validation performance.','models'),
('Iteration / epoch / tree','An SGD epoch is a pass over samples. A boosting iteration adds a boosting stage. In multiclass LightGBM, a stage normally contains one tree per class, so 1,508 rounds should not be casually called 1,508 individual trees.','models'),
('Early-stopping patience','How long training can continue without improving the monitored validation metric. Here LightGBM patience is 100 tuning rounds; its best iteration is retained.','models'),
('Hyperparameter tuning','Choose settings outside the fitted parameter updates. We compared two LightGBM capacities and several families, with early stopping; this was not an exhaustive search.','models'),
('Cross-validation','Fit/evaluate on several fold arrangements to summarize performance. Our final notebook takes one nested grouped split; the separate milestone CV study is a different experiment.','split'),
('Accuracy','Fraction of all rows classified correctly. On the audit this is 0.741498. It weights rows equally, rather than giving each tier equal weight.','audit'),
('Precision / recall','Precision asks how reliable a predicted class is; recall asks how much of the true class was recovered. Compute each using a one-class-versus-the-rest view.','audit'),
('F1 / macro / weighted','F1 is the harmonic mean of precision and recall. Macro F1 averages class F1 equally; weighted F1 weights by support; micro aggregation pools decisions. These are different summaries.','audit'),
('Support','The count of true examples for a class in an evaluation set. Tier 3 has 12,210 audit rows; it is not the number predicted as tier 3.','audit'),
('Confusion matrix','Rows are true labels and columns are predictions in our plot. Off-diagonal cells are mistakes; a true-normalized diagonal entry is that class’s recall.','audit'),
('Permutation importance','The score decrease after shuffling a column while keeping the model fixed. Small negative values mean a shuffle helped slightly in this diagnostic; they are not proof that the feature is universally harmful.','audit'),
('Correlation vs causation','Features can move together without one causing the target. A high correlation or importance does not establish a causal price effect.','eda'),
('Class imbalance','Some labels occur more often. Macro F1 avoids giving a class less weight simply because it has fewer rows, but does not automatically fix its learning difficulty.','audit'),
('Seed','A starting value for pseudo-random operations. The final seed is 173; the inner split uses 174. Reproducibility still depends on data, library/runtime and execution details.','split'),
('Distribution shift','The relationship or input distribution changes between evaluation and deployment. A different public score may reflect a different hidden subset; the score gap alone does not identify its cause.','refit'),
]

VIVA=[
('Explain the whole project in a minute.','I predict one of four workspace price tiers from supplied tabular attributes. I audited missingness and duplicate profiles, made grouped fit/tune/audit partitions, fitted reusable preprocessing on fitting rows, compared a linear classifier, Extra Trees and two boosting approaches, chose the 63-leaf LightGBM by tuning macro F1, evaluated it on audit, and refitted the frozen recipe on all labels before code submission.','load'),
('What do X and y mean?','X contains the predictors; y is the price_tier label. The target is removed before predictor-profile hashing and modelling. space_id is kept for output alignment but not used as a model feature.','load'),
('Why not treat tiers 1–4 as a regression target?','The required output is a class label and the evaluation is classification-based. Although tiers have an order, this notebook uses multiclass classification rather than an explicit ordinal or continuous-rent model.','load'),
('What did EDA actually change?','It established which columns needed cleaning/imputation, exposed repeated predictor profiles, and showed categorical and capacity associations. Those observations motivated grouped validation and careful feature representation; they do not prove any one engineering choice caused a score gain.','eda'),
('How many duplicates did you find?','Zero duplicate space IDs and 1,160 duplicated raw predictor profiles after excluding the ID. That count describes duplicate occurrences found by pandas, not necessarily 1,160 distinct duplicate groups.','eda'),
('Explain the exact 70/10/20 split arithmetic.','The outer splitter keeps one of five folds for audit: about 20%. One of eight folds from the remaining 80% becomes tuning: about 10% of the original total. The remaining seven eighths of that 80% is about 70% fitting. Groups make exact row counts slightly different from ideal fractions.','split'),
('Are your reported final scores five-fold CV averages?','No. next(...) takes one outer and one inner split. The final scores come from a fixed holdout arrangement. The separate M4 study used grouped CV and must not be described as the final notebook’s evaluation.','split'),
('Why can a label-free scaler still leak data?','Its means and scales can reflect evaluation rows. That changes preprocessing using information from outside fitting data. I fit the baseline preprocessor on F and only transform T and A until the final refit.','features'),
('What state does your feature builder learn?','The 99.5th-percentile caps for term fields, the facilities vocabulary with fitting frequency at least 20, and its token-to-column index. Other cleaning and derived-feature formulas are fixed transformation rules.','features'),
('Why median imputation and a missingness flag?','The median provides a simple fitting-set value and is less sensitive to extremes than the mean. A flag lets the model distinguish originally missing values from genuinely observed values near the median. Native boosting branches use their own missing handling.','features'),
('What does the facilities parser do?','It safely tries JSON and literal parsing, accepts lists of simple values, strips tokens and removes repeated entries. Malformed values get an explicit flag. Fitting selects sufficiently frequent tokens; transforming uses that frozen multi-hot layout.','features'),
('Why use log1p instead of log?','log1p is defined at zero and compresses heavy positive tails. For signed time intervals, sign(days) × log1p(abs(days)) preserves the direction of the interval. I do not claim it makes all distributions Gaussian.','features'),
('What happens to a brand-new category at inference?','The baseline OneHotEncoder handles unknown values without learning new columns. The LightGBM frame uses the already fitted category levels, so an unseen value becomes missing. CatBoost receives native strings. I do not fit a new vocabulary on test rows.','features'),
('Did you scale Extra Trees?','Yes. It reused the same baseline matrix as the linear model, including numeric standardization. Trees do not require that scaling; using it here was a shared preprocessing choice. The LightGBM and CatBoost branches use their own representations.','features'),
('Why keep separate input representations for models?','The linear baseline needs numeric encoded inputs; sparse one-hot features suit it. LightGBM can consume fitted categorical columns, and CatBoost can use categorical strings. Fair comparison means the same split and metric, not necessarily identical internal representations.','models'),
('How does SGDClassifier differ from gradient boosting?','SGD updates coefficients of a linear classifier using stochastic optimization. Boosting builds an additive collection of trees. Both can optimize classification losses, but they represent different kinds of patterns and learn them differently.','models'),
('Why is the linear model a useful baseline?','It is fast and tests what an additive model can capture with our representation. It reached tuning macro F1 0.629179. The stronger tree-based results suggest useful nonlinear interactions, but the comparison alone does not isolate exactly which interactions matter.','models'),
('How are Extra Trees and a random forest different?','Both combine randomized trees. Extra Trees uses more randomized split thresholds; a standard random forest searches candidate thresholds more directly and commonly uses bootstrap samples. Our Extra Trees configuration uses its default bootstrap=False.','models'),
('Explain boosting without saying it just memorizes wrong rows.','At each round it computes information about how the current predictions affect the training loss, fits a tree contribution to improve that loss, and adds a learning-rate-scaled update. For multiclass classification, this is more precise than saying it fits ordinary residual labels.','models'),
('What do 63 leaves and 1,508 iterations mean?','63 limits terminal regions per tree; it is a capacity setting. 1,508 is the best boosting round found on tuning data. Multiclass LightGBM generally builds a tree for each class per round, so iterations and individual-tree counts are different.','models'),
('How was regularization tested?','The second LightGBM setting reduces leaves from 63 to 31 and raises min_child_samples from 50 to 100. It reduced training F1 and the train–tune gap, but tuning F1 also fell to 0.728382. That did not justify replacing the tuning winner.','models'),
('Why use macro F1 instead of only accuracy?','Accuracy gives every row equal weight. Macro F1 computes F1 per tier and averages the four equally, so weak performance on a tier remains visible. The selected model’s audit macro F1 is 0.738081 and its accuracy is 0.741498.','audit'),
('Why not optimize F1 directly as the loss?','The standard tree-boosting training objective uses differentiable multiclass log loss. F1 depends on discrete predicted labels and aggregate counts. I monitor tuning macro F1 for selection while the learner optimizes its training objective.','models'),
('Why is tier 3 the main weakness?','It has the lowest recorded audit F1, about 0.5920, and recall about 0.5684. The confusion matrix shows where its rows are predicted instead. This establishes a measured weakness; it does not by itself establish the cause.','audit'),
('What does the confusion-matrix diagonal mean?','In the true-normalized plot, each row is all real examples of one tier. The diagonal is the fraction correctly predicted as that tier, which is recall. It is not precision, because precision conditions on a prediction column.','audit'),
('How reliable is your permutation-importance plot?','It is a diagnostic on 2,500 sampled audit rows with two repeats per feature. zone, metro and snapshot_span_days have the largest recorded drops. Correlation between features and sampling noise limit interpretation; I did not use it to retune on audit.','audit'),
('Did you remove all features with negative importance?','No. A small negative mean can result from sampling variation or interactions. The final notebook reports the diagnostic and retains the frozen model specification. Deleting features based on this audit plot would create a new selection decision.','audit'),
('What does the train–tune gap say about the winner?','Training macro F1 0.9443 is much higher than tuning 0.7355, so the training score overstates expected new-row performance. Regularization and early stopping help control this. The audit result supports this particular frozen choice, not a claim that there is no overfitting.','models'),
('Can you promise performance on a new operator or city?','No. The split groups exact predictor profiles. It does not explicitly keep operators or metros disjoint. An operator-disjoint or geographic transfer evaluation would answer a different and harder generalization question.','audit'),
('Was there any audit exposure before model selection?','The initial EDA was on the full labelled table. Audit rows were subsequently excluded from fitted preprocessing, training, early stopping and winner selection. I describe that boundary accurately rather than claiming the audit was untouched during all analysis.','split'),
('Why refit on all labels after reporting audit performance?','Once the recipe is frozen, all labels can train the competition model. I reuse the selected settings and round count. The earlier audit score belongs to the earlier fit-stage model; the refitted model is assessed by the competition submission.','refit'),
('How did you ensure submission rows were aligned?','I copied the sample template, checked its ID column equals test.space_id in order, assigned integer predictions, asserted the test row count and valid labels, checked for missing values, and wrote CSV without a pandas index.','refit'),
('What were the final actual scores?','For the selected LightGBM: tune macro F1 0.735466, audit macro F1 0.738081. The registered version-3 code submission 57777975 received public score 0.70158. They measure different partitions; I do not substitute one for another.','refit'),
('Did the final notebook use stacking or the M3 selected features?','No. M3’s L1-selected 200-input classifier and M4’s voting/stacking study are separate experiments. The final registered notebook selected a single LightGBM model from five configurations across four model families.','models'),
('What would you improve next?','First test the generalization boundary with operator-disjoint splits and investigate tier-3 errors. Any feature or model changes should be selected on a new development protocol, with a fresh untouched evaluation if I want an independent result. I would not call audit-driven changes an unchanged final model.','audit'),
]

OUTPUT_NOTES={
2:[('Environment and shape check','The shapes describe rows × columns. The training table has one extra column: price_tier.'),('Three real training rows','This is an input-data preview. A NaN means missing, and category strings are nominal labels. These rows are not the sample submission.')],
4:[('Duplicate checks','0 repeated IDs does not imply 0 repeated predictor profiles. The 1,160 profile duplicates motivate grouped splitting.'),('Original missingness table','missing_fraction is the share of rows with no recorded value. 0.766592 for borough is about 76.66%, not 0.77%.'),('Original EDA: three panels','Left: tier on x, labelled-row count on y. Middle: missing fraction on x, column names on y. Right: tier on x, max_seats on y; tier 4 has a higher capacity median, with overlapping distributions. Hidden outlier markers were not removed from the dataset.'),('Metro versus tier proportions','Each row is one metro; columns are price tiers. Values are proportions within the metro. MTR-07 has 0.754 in tier 1. This is descriptive association, not prediction accuracy or a guarantee for new metros.'),('Space class versus tier proportions','This table shows the first 15 category rows, not the complete set. Some small categories can show extreme proportions from very few examples. Do not infer certainty from a row containing 1.000.'),('Access type versus tier proportions','For shared_zone, about 84.9% of these labelled rows are tier 1. That shows an association in the supplied data; it does not prove the access type causes a price tier.'),('Original numeric correlation heatmap','The same numeric features appear on both axes. Redder cells mean stronger positive Pearson correlation; diagonal values are 1 by definition. Several related count/score/term fields form blocks. The target and ID were removed from this heatmap, so this is not feature–target importance.')],
6:[('Exact split sizes','These counts sum to 279,746. Groups are indivisible, so actual proportions need not be exactly 70%, 10% and 20%.'),('Class mixture after splitting','Each row is a partition and sums to about 1. Similar proportions show that the splitter broadly preserved the target mixture; they do not prove every possible distribution is matched.')],
8:[('Dimensions after feature construction','227 is the engineered feature count on fitting rows. 1,165 is the baseline matrix width after encoding/imputation indicators. 144 is the fit-only facilities vocabulary size. The final full-data refit relearns its own preprocessing.')],
10:[('Actual training and early-stopping log','LightGBM’s valid_0 is the tuning data passed in eval_set. CatBoost’s log calls its eval_set “test”; in this cell that means tuning data, not the hidden competition test. Best LightGBM rounds are 1,508 and 1,310. These logs are saved execution output.'),('Tuning comparison used for selection','The table is sorted by tune_macro_f1. Training F1 diagnoses fit, while fit_seconds reports this run’s elapsed estimator fitting time. CatBoost was slower here; that is not a universal benchmark.'),('Winner frozen here','This print occurs before the audit cell. It records the selection from tuning macro F1, not a choice made from later audit or leaderboard results.')],
12:[('Recorded model comparison including audit','Audit columns are appended to the already recorded tuning comparison. The winner remains the earlier tuning choice; this step does not retune it.'),('Original per-class classification report','Read each tier as one class versus the rest. Support is the count of true rows. Tier 3 is the weakest recorded class: F1 about 0.5920. Macro averages weight each class equally; weighted averages weight by support.'),('Original normalized audit confusion matrix','True tier is on the vertical axis; predicted tier is on the horizontal axis. Each true-label row is normalized to 1. Diagonal values are recall: tier 3 is about 0.57, not 57% precision. The interactive exact-count version follows below.'),('Metro error-rate diagnostic','count is the number of audit rows in the metro. error_rate is the proportion predicted incorrectly. Only groups with at least 100 rows are displayed; the table shows the highest rates, not every metro.'),('Space-class error-rate diagnostic','These are group error proportions among audit rows, again with count ≥100. An error rate difference is descriptive and does not establish the cause or a statistically reliable ordering.'),('Actual mistaken audit examples','truth and predicted disagree in these rows. These are selected errors, not a random sample from which to estimate overall performance.'),('Original permutation-importance plot','Horizontal length is the mean decrease in macro F1 after shuffling a feature on 2,500 sampled audit rows, with two repeats. zone, metro and snapshot_span_days lead. 0.161 means a score decrease in this diagnostic, not 16.1% causal responsibility or probability.')],
14:[('Recorded final-run summary','The tune/audit values are the earlier selection-stage measurements. They are not new scores of the full-data refitted object.'),('First five submission rows','These are test predictions with output column names ID and price_tier. The ID numbering resembles the training preview but belongs to the test table; do not confuse the two datasets.'),('Predicted tier distribution','The counts sum to 114,538. They describe model outputs on test data; they do not reveal the hidden true class distribution or prove prediction correctness.')]
}

